In this lesson we'll look at the Law of Total Probability, a neat trick for finding the chance of an event when it's awkward to calculate directly. Here's the idea. Imagine your whole sample space is a pizza. You slice it up into non-overlapping pieces, and every possible outcome lands in exactly one slice. Those slices are your partition events, often called B one, B two, B three, and so on. The rule says that the probability of some event A equals the sum over every slice of the chance of B i times the chance of A given that slice has already happened. In plain English, you split the question into cases, handle each one, and add the pieces back together. Why is this useful? Because the conditional pieces are often easy to compute, like reading a "given" branch off a probability tree. You just multiply each branch by how likely that case is, then sum them up to get the overall chance of A. Now let's see it in action.